<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Barcid Iberia</h1><p class="st-meta">237 BCE – 206 BCE · capital Qart Hadasht</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Carthaginian domain built in Iberia by Hamilcar Barca and his heirs, the silver-rich base from which Hannibal set out to invade Italy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the First Punic War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in 237 BCE. Landing at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gadir">Gadir</a>, he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley and its mines. After his death in about 229 BCE, his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal</a> extended Carthaginian power by diplomacy and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a>, later Carthago Nova, around 227 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> was run almost as a family dominion of the Barcids, nominally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from mines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> paid for armies of Iberian, Celtiberian and African troops. Alliances with local rulers were sealed by marriages, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal's</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himilce">Himilce of Castulo</a>. In 226 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fixed a northern limit.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, in command from 221 BCE, took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saguntum">Saguntum</a> in 219 BCE, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a> in 209 BCE and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilipa">Ilipa</a> in 206 BCE, ending Carthaginian rule. The territory became the Roman provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Ulterior</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barcid-iberia.jpg" alt="Barcid Iberia" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dama_de_Elche_(M.A.N._Madrid)_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>237 BCE – 206 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qart Hadasht</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamilcar Barca, Hasdrubal the Fair, Hannibal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Punic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carthaginian Iberia, Barcid Spain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-222&amp;focus=barcid-iberia" aria-label="Barcid Iberia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4655 825 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barcid Iberia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4975 1081L4993 1013M4975 1081L5259 1106M4975 1081L5031 967M4975 1081L4881 1072M4975 1081L4931 1085M4975 1081L4910 1067M4975 1081L4901 1007"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4993 1013h0M5259 1106h0M5031 967h0M4881 1072h0M4931 1085h0M4910 1067h0M4901 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot